A||||19||BluByYou||Ultimate Dogbone
Z||000000||BluByYou||07-21-99||09:02 AM||||I think I'm going to try an "ultimate".  My car only has 52500 miles, but if you juice the engine (from the engine compartment) you can see engine move plenty - mostly from the bushings in the old dog bone.  Will an Ultimate lock the engine right in place?  I realize I have the lower mounts to be concerned with as well, but I thought I start with the simplest first.
Z||000001||lowCG||07-21-99||11:43 AM||||Is your car a 4cyl?<br>If so,the vibrations are like 5 times as great as with the six,and I found the new replacement(dealer)part to work well in both controlling movement,and vibration in my old 4cyl car.<br>Just made a stiff one for my six cylinder car,can't tell much difference in the comfort area,as the engine is so smooth,works great. 
Z||000002||BluByYou||07-21-99||11:56 AM||||Yes, it's 6 cyl. GT.  The biggest movement I feel is in shifting.  Especially 1st to second.  I have an automatic, and I think the engine flexes quite a bit during that high torque shift.  I wonder if the dogbone would "solidify" that shift (power to the ground).
Z||000003||batboy||07-21-99||10:00 PM||||The factory dogbones are known to wear out fairly rapidly. I replaced mine about 50,000 miles ago and it needs replaced again. I'm getting an ultimate dogbone for my next one.
Z||000004||PaUL||07-22-99||01:54 AM||||Ive got the stock dogbone on my 2.8 v6. Ive noticed that the rubber bushing closest to the engine is where most of the movement occurs. Changing just the one bushing to a polyurethane one should eliminate most of the movement. I wish I had a friend with a fiero so I could split the cost of a set of poly dogbone bushings. 
Z||000005||Bruce||07-22-99||10:15 AM||||I was experiencing substantial engine vibration and noise until I purchased the Dog-bone from the Fiero Store. It eliminated it immediately. When I replaced the stock mount, I notice the rubber bushings were shot. It's a wise investment for $44, especially since that mount eventually effects the other engine mounts. It's not even that tough to install.
Z||000006||Pretender||07-25-99||04:51 PM||||whats the install like?..i'm also thinking about getting one., it looks really easy but sometimes that the hardest thing to do..know what i mean?...:o)
Z||000007||theogre||07-25-99||04:58 PM||||the stock spec dogbone for 4 cyl is a pile of trash to say the least. there is nearly 2 inches of travel in a new one. It gets worse quickly. It certain speeds it allows oscilation of the motor. Ever feel like something was whacking you in the back at low engine load? The dogbone is part of the cause.<p>I just replaced the stock bone with another stock bone 20K miles ago. I'll be replacing it agian soon with urethane. It's already starting to tear apart.
Z||000008||batboy||07-25-99||07:01 PM||||Pretender, I don't remember if you have a 4 or a 6 cylinder engine. The 2.5 dogbone is super easy to replace. I've never done it on a V6, but I'm assuming that it would be fairly straight forward too. I believe with the V6 you can just replace the bushings. Ogre's right about the 4 cylinder ones being junk, my last new one only lasted about 50,000 miles (mostly highway). If you're doing a lot of stop and go driving, expect to replace those stock dogbones even more often.
Z||000009||Bruce||07-25-99||09:25 PM||||On the V6 the removal and installation is very simple, but you have to get a pry bar to align the engine with the bolt fittings. You'll understand once you take the old dog bone out. Anytime you hear that "clunking" sound right behind you, it is most probably due to the stock mount. I would imagine the rubber bushings don't have a very long life, given the engine heat. 
Z||000010||batboy||07-25-99||09:39 PM||||With the car in gear or park (depending on the tranny) you should be able to just lean against the back of the car to realign to bolt holes, you'll be surprised how much that engine moves around without the dogbone connected.
Z||000011||Pretender||07-25-99||11:14 PM||||its a 6cyl..i think it's going bad but i've never heard any movement, but when i get on it good you can feel the engine movement when it shifts into second. with my rear tires(half bald) i can chirp second, but it will not, absolutely spin in first. *88auto*.
Z||000012||lowCG||07-26-99||12:32 AM||||Yeah,come to think about it,I guess I never really got on it much in my 4 cyl. car,hardly worth it I figured.I just drove it like a proper commuter car(except for the corners),put about 50K on it,got the dealer dog bone the second day I owned it,so I suppose the durability is dependent on how you drive.It's also important to never let any oil get on this part,or extra movement will come quickly after.
Z||000013||GREENDANO||07-27-99||06:46 PM||||Has anyone considered just replacing the bushings with the polyurethane ones that are available?  I think they're $25.  That's way cheaper than the Fiero Store price.  Go here for the link<br>---&gt;http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm<br>  Dan-O
Z||000014||theogre||07-27-99||08:28 PM||||That's definatly an option.... F.S. also has the bushing only option, for about the same price. I haven't decided which to use. I kind of like the look of the stock AL bone frame. Far as I can tell, the bushing only is actually a set of 4 buchings. You'd about have to do that since the AL would never take pressing it. Can't be too hard to put in.<p>As for 4 cyl performance.... I get to 65+ just fine in my 87. Maybe it's the fact it's an 87, but I drive the hell out of it.... I'll put 25-30K miles on it this year. (I've done 20K since last october already.)
Z||000015||GREENDANO||07-27-99||08:32 PM||||I have the bushings I bought locally from Prothane I believe or maybe it was suspension techniques.  They were only $24.  Last time I looked the ultimate Dogbone was $42?  That's a big price diference to me.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000016||sid1200||07-28-99||01:21 PM||||is the 88 V6 dogbone (stock) or mounting the same as the 85-87, I heard it was upgraded and is a lot better. So was wondering if the ultimate would help an 88, or if stock was good enough.
Z||000017||mwbackus||07-28-99||03:26 PM||||The 88 V6 uses a different dogbone then the previous years and the bushings are not easy to remove.  I use a Fiero Store poly dogbone on my formula and have had good results so far.  One thing I found that helped even more than the dogbone was extending the bump stop on the rear trans mount by about 1/4". If you pull the driver side rear wheel and look at the rear trans mount you will see the bump stop that I am referring to.  I am not sure if this bump stop was used in previous years, but this little $5 mod (along with the poly bone) eliminates nearly all the flex in the power train on the 88's.<p>Mike
Z||000018||sluppy123||07-29-99||06:35 PM||||To fix my dog bone problem on my GT I pulled the stock on off, removed the cheap old rubber bushing, bored out about .050" from the holes and pressed in a set a poly bushings I found at the parts store."I think they were for a Ford truck". Worked out great!! 
Z||000019||lowCG||07-29-99||11:21 PM||||Yeah,that's pretty much what I did,except I was waiting for someone and needed something to do in about 20 minutes,so I dug around in my bump stop collection,took a die grinder to the sharp web-like violation within the casting,cut off a few pieces of synthetic rubber,and squeezed it back into my car.
